# The one local environment file. Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in what you
# need.
#
# It is read by three things, and they agree because they read the same file:
#
#   task / Taskfile.yml     dotenv: ['.env'] — every target
#   task cluster:deploy     through devspace.yaml's vars, into helm values
#   task cluster:dev        the same block, so dev and deploy cannot drift
#   pnpm dev (the editor)   apps/platform/next.config.ts, via @next/env
#
# There used to be a second one at apps/platform/.env for the editor. It is gone,
# and deliberately: that directory is the platform image's build context and its
# DevSpace sync path, so a developer with SSO configured baked their OIDC client
# secret into every locally built image and synced it into the running pod. This
# file is in neither, and .dockerignore now excludes .env everywhere regardless.
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL. A checkout with no .env builds and deploys a
# working cluster; each variable below buys one thing, and says what.
#
# A deployed cluster reads none of this directly: the chart supplies every
# service's environment, and credentials arrive as Secrets. This file is how your
# machine tells the chart what to put in them.

# --- The embedding server ----------------------------------------------------
# Turns text into vectors, so that searching agent memory ranks by MEANING rather
# than by words. Set the key and `task cluster:deploy` brings it up alongside
# everything else; leave it unset and there is no embedding server at all —
# search still works, it matches text, which is a supported way to run rather
# than a degraded one.
#
# The key is here rather than in helm/values-k3d.yaml because it is a credential
# and that file is committed. The other three are here beside it because the key
# alone is not enough to reach anything: an OpenRouter key against the OpenAI
# default authenticates nowhere, and that failure arrives as a 401 from a
# provider you did not pick rather than as a configuration mistake.
#
# Only the key turns the server on. The rest default to a coherent OpenAI setup,
# so setting the key alone is valid — as long as it IS an OpenAI key.

# EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=sk-...

# Which provider connector to build. Default: llm-openai
# Also valid: llm-gemini, llm-openrouter.
# NOT llm-anthropic — Anthropic has no embeddings API, so the connector does not
# implement the capability and the server refuses to start rather than failing on
# the first call.
# EMBEDDINGS_CONNECTOR_TYPE=llm-openai

# The embedding model, provider-specific. Default: text-embedding-3-small
#
# Note the ids differ by provider even for the same model. OpenRouter prefixes
# the vendor, so the same model is "openai/text-embedding-3-small" there and
# "text-embedding-3-small" on OpenAI directly.
# EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# The stored vector width. Default: 1536
#
# It must match the vector(N) columns in sql/schema.sql, which are indexed and so
# fixed at schema time — changing this alone will not change those. It is
# REQUESTED of the provider (most embeddings APIs take a dimensions parameter); a
# model that cannot produce this width fails the call and says so.
# EMBEDDINGS_DIMENSIONS=1536

# --- Using an OpenRouter key -------------------------------------------------
# The combination that trips people up, since the default provider is OpenAI:
#
#   EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...
#   EMBEDDINGS_CONNECTOR_TYPE=llm-openrouter
#   EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small

# =============================================================================
# The editor (apps/platform)
# =============================================================================
# Read by `pnpm dev` through next.config.ts, and — for the first four — passed
# into the cluster by devspace.yaml when you run `task cluster:deploy` or
# `task cluster:dev`. Setting OIDC_ISSUER is what turns single sign-on on in
# both places at once; leaving it empty leaves the editor open, which is the
# ordinary way to run locally.

# --- Sessions ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the session cookie. Required whenever OIDC is on; generate one with
#   openssl rand -hex 32
AUTH_SECRET=

# The canonical base URL Auth.js builds sign-in redirects and OAuth callbacks
# against. Without it, trustHost infers the host from request headers — behind
# the k3d loadbalancer that arrives as 0.0.0.0:3000 and the redirect breaks.
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Single sign-on (optional) ----------------------------------------------
# Leave OIDC_ISSUER empty and authentication is off: the platform is open and
# every request runs as a local user. That is the ordinary way to run locally.
#
# Register {AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/oidc with your IdP before turning it on —
# the Auth.js provider id is `oidc`.
# OIDC_ISSUER=https://idp.example.com
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# How the sign-in button names the provider ("Sign in with …"). Empty renders
# "OIDC"; the logo defaults to the issuer's favicon.
# OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=
# OIDC_PROVIDER_LOGO=
# OIDC_SCOPES=
# Overrides for an IdP whose discovery document is absent or wrong. Unset, the
# endpoints come from {OIDC_ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration.
# OIDC_AUTHORIZATION_URL=
# OIDC_TOKEN_URL=
# OIDC_USERINFO_URL=
# OIDC_JWKS_URL=
# OIDC_PROVIDER_ID=

# Who may write. Unset means every role except platform:monitor, which is the
# role that means "looks, and nothing else". Set it to narrow that further.
# AUTH_WRITE_ROLES=platform:admin,platform:operator
#
# Roles are no longer read from an id-token claim: sign-in trades the provider's
# token with iam for a platform one, and the roles on it are rows in this
# platform's own database. AUTH_ROLES_CLAIM is gone, and so is the arrangement
# where the identity provider decided who could do what here.

# --- Services the editor talks to (local `pnpm dev` only) --------------------
# These have NO defaults in code — each falls back to an empty string, and an
# empty base URL fails at the point of use rather than at startup, so the symptom
# is a feature that looks broken rather than configuration that looks missing. In
# the cluster the chart supplies them; for a local `pnpm dev` they are yours.
#
# Only the orchestrator is published to the host by k3d (deploy/k3d/cluster.yaml
# maps 3000 and 8090, and nothing else). Everything below it is ClusterIP, so
# reaching it from a local dev server means forwarding the port first:
#
#   kubectl -n octo-dev port-forward svc/octo-observability 8091:8091
#   kubectl -n octo-dev port-forward svc/octo-nats 8222:8222
#
# Left commented for that reason: set them once you have the forward, not before.
ORCHESTRATOR_URL=http://localhost:8090
# The observability service — the Logs, Traces and Metrics pages, and the
# retention settings.
# OBSERVABILITY_URL=http://localhost:8091
# The NATS monitoring API, which is what the Queues page reads.
# NATS_MONITOR_URL=http://localhost:8222
# NATS itself, for live deployment-status push. Unset means no live updates and
# the pages fall back to what they fetched — the least missed of the four.
# NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222

# Where this app tells others it lives, both optional.
# PLATFORM_URL is used to build absolute links on the gateway error page.
# MCP_RESOURCE_URL is the OAuth resource identifier advertised to MCP clients;
# unset leaves the MCP endpoint unadvertised.
# PLATFORM_URL=http://localhost:3000
# MCP_RESOURCE_URL=

# The documentation site the in-app links point at. Unset uses the published one.
# OCTO_DOCS_URL=

# ── The iam service ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The platform signs people in through iam: it trades the identity provider's
# token for one this platform signed, carrying the caller's octo user id and
# their roles.
#
# IAM_URL is where the editor reaches it. Unset, sign-in cannot complete — which
# is on purpose, since a session with no platform token would look signed in and
# be able to call nothing.
# IAM_URL=http://localhost:8093
#
# The rest of this block is for running `task iam:run` locally.
#
# It shares DATABASE_URL and, when SSO is on, OIDC_ISSUER / OIDC_CLIENT_ID with
# the settings above: one install has one database and one identity provider.
#
# IAM_ISSUER is the `iss` iam stamps into every token and the base its discovery
# document advertises, so it must be the address callers actually reach it at. In
# the cluster the chart renders it; locally that is wherever you are running it.
# Unset leaves token signing off, and POST /auth reports itself unavailable.
# IAM_ISSUER=http://localhost:8093
#
# The rest are optional and default inside the service: audience "octo", tokens
# valid 1h, signing keys replaced every 720h. There is deliberately no signing
# key to set — iam generates, stores and rotates its own.
# IAM_AUDIENCE=octo
# IAM_TOKEN_TTL=1h
# IAM_KEY_LIFETIME=720h
#
# How long past its expiry a platform token can still be traded for a fresh one.
# It is what lets somebody who stepped away from a tab come back to a working
# session rather than a sign-in page; past it, they sign in again.
# IAM_REFRESH_GRACE=10m
#
# Other audiences this install answers to, comma-separated. OIDC_CLIENT_ID is
# always accepted and never needs listing. The one that matters in a deployment
# is the /mcp resource identifier, since an MCP client's token is minted for that
# rather than for the editor's client id.
# IAM_ACCEPTED_AUDIENCES=http://localhost:3000/mcp
